nio: skip old-task termination on the boot's first exec

The launchd exec now gets past load_machfile (the halt there is skipped
on the boot thread), but __mac_execve's success cleanup still calls
task_terminate_internal(old_task).  The boot exec's old_task was retargeted
to the init task, which still holds the not-yet-scheduled cloneproc thread,
so termination blocks waiting for that thread just like task_complete_halt
did.

There is no previous task to reap on the boot's first exec.  Skip this
cleanup when running on the boot thread (current_task is kernel_task);
normal user execs still terminate their old task.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
@@ -4666,6 +4666,8 @@ __mac_execve(proc_t p, struct __mac_execve_args *uap, int32_t *retval __unused)
 	 * result in loosing the SIGKILL status.
 	 */
-	if (task_did_exec(old_task)) {
+	/* nio: boot's first exec has no old task to reap; terminating the init
+	 * task would block on its unscheduled thread. */
+	if (task_did_exec(old_task) && current_task() != kernel_task) {
 		/* Terminate the current task, since exec will start in new task */
 		task_terminate_internal(old_task);
 	}
